<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/equations-and-inequalities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An equation is a balanced scale. On the left pan sit some weights, on the right some others,
    and the bar tells you the two sides are <em>equal</em>. The letter — the unknown, x — is a
    weight whose value you don't know yet. Solving an equation means discovering it, and the
    only licence you ever have is this: <strong>whatever you do to one side, do to the other</strong>.
    Add 3 to both, subtract 5 from both, divide both by 2 — the balance holds and the equation
    stays true. Do something to one side only and the scale tips: the statement is no longer
    equivalent to what you started with. That single principle, applied repeatedly, is all of
    "solving".
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does this matter beyond the classroom? Because equations are how the world is written
    down. A phone bill, a braking distance, a population forecast, a mortgage: each is a
    relationship between quantities, and an equation is the general form of that relationship —
    solved once, reused forever, with the numbers swapped in per case. Algebra's letter is the
    genius move: <strong>x</strong> stands for "whatever the number is", so reasoning can be
    done about values you haven't met yet. Inequalities are the same story one level up in
    realism: rarely does the world ask "exactly equal?" — it asks "at least", "at most",
    "within". The asker obeys the same balance rules, with one crucial twist.
  </p>

  <h2>Algebra: the language and its manipulation</h2>
  <p>
    Before equations can be solved, they must be written and tidied. Algebra's grammar is
    compact: letters are numbers, juxtaposition is multiplication, and expressions like
    3x + 2x − 7 are sentences describing computations in general. The core manipulation skills
    are few and mechanical. <strong>Collecting like terms</strong> (3x + 2x = 5x — you can add
    3 of a thing and 2 of a thing; you cannot add 3 apples to 2 apples-squared).
    <strong>Expanding brackets</strong> using distribution: 3(x − 2) = 3x − 6, because
    multiplication distributes over addition. <strong>Factorising</strong> runs it backwards —
    3x − 6 = 3(x − 2) — and matters because factors reveal structure: in
    x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3), the factored form shows instantly that the expression is zero when
    x = 3 or x = −3. <strong>Substitution</strong> evaluates an expression for a chosen value —
    the bridge between general rule and specific case.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order of operations (brackets, powers, then × and ÷, then + and −) is not a school
    ritual; it is a convention that makes the written form unambiguous, and every manipulation
    rule is really the statement that two written forms compute the same number. Fluency here
    is like touch-typing for everything that follows: quadratics, calculus and analysis are
    these moves composed with new objects.
  </p>

  <h2>Equations: peeling the onion</h2>
  <p>
    Solving follows one strategy: <strong>undo in reverse order</strong>, stripping the
    unknown like an onion. Worked example — solve 2(x + 5) = 3x − 4. Expand: 2x + 10 = 3x − 4.
    Subtract 2x from both sides: 10 = x − 4. Add 4 to both sides: 14 = x. Check by substituting
    back: 2(14 + 5) = 38 and 3(14) − 4 = 38 — balanced, so x = 14 is correct, and the ten-second
    check catches most algebra slips ever made.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two refinements matter. First, <strong>never divide by the unknown</strong>: from
    x² = x, dividing by x keeps only x = 1 and silently discards x = 0 — factorise instead
    (x(x − 1) = 0) and both answers survive. Second, equations with the unknown twice
    (<strong>quadratics</strong>) generally need factoring or the quadratic formula
    x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, which solves ax² + bx + c = 0 for any coefficients; the ±
    announces that quadratics usually have <em>two</em> answers, and the discriminant b² − 4ac
    tells you how many real ones exist (two, one, or none). <strong>Simultaneous equations</strong>
    extend the idea to several unknowns: two facts, two letters, eliminate one letter by
    combining the equations — the same balance logic applied to a system of scales.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the one rule that surprises everyone — dividing by a negative flips the inequality" widget="equationStep" config={ineqCfg} />

  <h2>Inequalities: the balance with a twist</h2>
  <p>
    An inequality like 3x − 6 > 5x + 4 is solved with the identical moves — and as the widget
    shows, exactly one of them differs. Adding or subtracting anything, and multiplying or
    dividing by a <em>positive</em> number, preserves the comparison: bigger stays bigger.
    But multiplying or dividing by a <em>negative</em> number flips the direction, because
    multiplying by −1 turns "greater" into "less": 3 > 2 is true, yet −3 &lt; −2. Forgetting the
    flip is the single most common inequality error in existence. Solutions are ranges, not
    points, and are written with the same symbols: x &lt; −5 means everything left of −5 on the
    number line; x ≥ 2 includes the endpoint, x > 2 does not. <strong>Double inequalities</strong>
    such as −1 ≤ x &lt; 4 sandwich a value between bounds — the everyday mathematics of tolerances,
    speed limits and medication doses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "x is a number hiding behind a letter, and there is exactly
    one right answer." Equations can have none (x + 1 = x), one, two, or infinitely many
    (x + x = 2x is true for every x). The letter is a placeholder for whatever makes the
    statement true, and "how many solutions?" is always a genuine question, not a formality.
  </p>

  <h2>Rearranging formulas: solving before you have numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The same balance logic solves a second everyday problem: making a different letter the
    subject of a formula. Physics hands you v = u + at and asks for t; the recipe is to
    treat the other letters as numbers and undo in reverse order. Subtract u from both
    sides: v − u = at. Divide by a: t = (v − u) ÷ a — and the check is substitution: feed
    in any values and confirm both forms agree (u = 0, a = 2, t = 3 gives v = 6 in both).
    Worked example with two steps: from A = ½bh, make h the subject — multiply both sides
    by 2 (2A = bh), divide by b (h = 2A/b). This skill is why the balance rule pays twice:
    once for finding unknowns, once for reshaping relationships before the numbers arrive.
    The one caution mirrors the equations section — dividing by a letter assumes it is not
    zero, and formulas where a letter can vanish (v² = u² + 2as, solving for a when s = 0)
    need that case handled separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Simultaneous equations: two facts, two unknowns</h2>
  <p>
    When two unknowns appear, one equation is a line of possible answers; two equations pin
    down a point. <strong>Elimination</strong> is the workhorse: solve 2x + y = 11 and
    x − y = 1 by adding the two equations (y cancels): 3x = 12, so x = 4, and substituting
    back gives y = 3. <strong>Substitution</strong> is the alternative: from the second
    equation y = x − 1, insert into the first: 2x + x − 1 = 11, so x = 4 again — two routes,
    one destination, and either can be checked in both original equations (8 + 3 = 11; 4 − 3 = 1).
    Graphically, each equation is a line and the answer is their intersection — which is
    also how word problems become solvable: translate the sentences into two equations, and
    the algebra does the rest. Inequalities extend to two dimensions as <em>regions</em> —
    y &lt; 2x + 1 is half a plane, shaded below the line, dashed where the boundary is
    excluded — the mathematics behind every "budget feasible" diagram in economics.
  </p>

  <h2>Functions and graphs: equations you can see</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>function</strong> is an equation promoted to a machine: an input goes in, a rule
    is applied, one output comes out — f(x) = 2x + 1 turns 3 into 7, every time. Writing f(x)
    instead of y makes the <em>dependence</em> explicit, which is how science thinks: distance
    as a function of time, cost as a function of quantity. The <strong>graph</strong> is the
    function's portrait: plot every input against its output and straight lines (2x + 1),
    parabolas (x²) and the whole zoo of curves become visible shapes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Graphs in practical situations are exactly these pictures with units attached. A distance–time
    graph's <strong>gradient is speed</strong> (steeper = faster; a flat stretch = stopped); a
    cost graph's gradient is price per unit; where two graphs <strong>intersect</strong> is the
    point where two options become equal — the break-even point, the moment one courier's flat
    fee beats the other's per-km rate. Solving an equation graphically means finding where the
    line crosses zero (for one equation) or where two lines cross (for a pair): the intersection
    <em>is</em> the solution, which is why the graphical picture is not decoration but a second,
    independent way of thinking. <strong>Sketching curves</strong> adds the qualitative habits:
    find where the curve crosses the axes, note the turning point of a parabola, and read the
    overall shape before fussing with points.
  </p>

  <h2>Sequences: patterns with a rule</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>sequence</strong> is a function whose inputs are 1, 2, 3, … — a pattern numbered.
    <strong>Arithmetic sequences</strong> add a fixed step each time (3, 7, 11, 15: step +4),
    with general term aₙ = a₁ + (n − 1)d; their graphs are points on a straight line.
    <strong>Geometric sequences</strong> multiply by a fixed ratio (2, 6, 18, 54: ×3), with
    aₙ = a₁ × rⁿ⁻¹; their graphs explode upward when r > 1 — the shape of compound interest
    and population growth. Sequences train exactly the skill equations need in bulk: staring
    at values and inferring the underlying rule, then trusting the rule beyond the values you
    can see.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>differential calculus</strong> is where graphs become dynamics. Differentiation
    asks a geometric question — what is the gradient of a curve at a single point? — and
    answers it with a limiting argument: take the gradient between two nearby points, then let
    the points approach. For f(x) = x², the gradient between x and x + h is (x + h)² − x² over
    h, which simplifies to 2x + h; as h shrinks to nothing, the gradient tends to 2x. So the
    parabola's slope at any point is twice its x-coordinate — a formula, not an estimate, and
    the master key to rates of change everywhere: velocity is the derivative of distance,
    marginal cost the derivative of cost, decay rate the derivative of population. That an
    infinite process (shrinking h forever) can land on a definite, finite number is one of the
    deepest ideas in mathematics, and it lives two steps downstream from the balance rule you
    learned above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two expert nuances close the loop. First, <strong>equivalence is the invariant that
    matters</strong>: each legal move transforms an equation into a new equation with the same
    solution set, and illegal moves (dividing by the unknown, squaring both sides without
    checking — which can insert solutions such as the extraneous root of √(x) = −3) change the
    set. Professionals track the solution set, not the ritual of the moves. Second, the
    <strong>inequality flip</strong> generalises: it is a statement about order-reversing maps,
    and the same idea reappears as taking reciprocals of positive numbers (2 &lt; 3 but ½ > ⅓) and
    in the behaviour of decreasing functions. When a later course tells you that
    log₁₀(x) &lt; log₁₀(y) implies x &lt; y, you already know why — the logarithm is increasing —
    and why the same statement with a negative multiplier would need the flip. Algebra is not
    a bag of tricks; it is one small, strict grammar for keeping equivalence alive.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Elementary Algebra and College Algebra; Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E2); standard school algebra curricula.</p>
</TopicPage>
